  <body>
    <nav class="nav">
      <a href="/" class="nav-link">← Back to Work</a>
      <div>
        <span class="nav-link">Keystrok / 2025</span>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <div class="container">
      <!-- Hero Spread -->
      <section class="hero-spread">
        <div class="hero-left">
          <div class="hero-number">01</div>
          <h1 class="hero-title">
            Keystrok: I designed the API-key security tool I couldn't find, then
            built and shipped it
          </h1>
          <p class="hero-subtitle">
            Self-hosted, open-source API key management that developers actually
            want to use
          </p>

          <div class="hero-links">
            <a href="https://github.com/Rootzreggae/keystrok">GitHub →</a>
            <a href="https://codeberg.org/windgodfist/keystrok">Codeberg →</a>
          </div>

          <div class="hero-meta-grid">
            <div class="meta-stat">
              <div class="meta-number">MIT</div>
              <div class="meta-label">Open source, no paywall</div>
            </div>
            <div class="meta-stat">
              <div class="meta-number">1 cmd</div>
              <div class="meta-label">Self-host with docker compose</div>
            </div>
            <div class="meta-stat">
              <div class="meta-number">0</div>
              <div class="meta-label">Secrets stored in plaintext</div>
            </div>
            <div class="meta-stat">
              <div class="meta-number">~2 yrs</div>
              <div class="meta-label">Research, design, and build</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-right">
          <img
            src="/images/keystrok/home.png"
            alt="Keystrok home: what needs attention, ranked by urgency"
            class="hero-image"
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Executive Summary -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">EXECUTIVE SUMMARY</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">From a real pain to a shipped product</h2>
          <p class="section-description">
            I kept running into the same problem managing API keys, researched
            whether other developers did too (they did), and designed and built
            a tool to fix it, end to end. Not a mockup: a working, self-hosted,
            open-source product with discovery, a tracked key ledger, guided
            rotation, and multi-user teams. This is how I work as a designer,
            spot a real problem, make opinionated calls, and actually ship it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Problem Section -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">02 — THE PROBLEM</span>
          <h2 class="section-title"
            >Nobody had made key management pleasant to do</h2
          >
        </div>

        <div class="problem-grid">
          <div class="problem-content">
            <h3>The gap</h3>
            <ul>
              <li
                >Leaked and stale credentials are one of the most common ways
                systems get breached</li
              >
              <li
                >Secrets platforms (Vault, Infisical) are heavyweight, and they
                store your keys</li
              >
              <li
                >Scanners (gitleaks, trufflehog) find secrets, then stop, no
                triage, no rotation</li
              >
              <li
                >Nobody owns the messy middle: what leaked, is it still live,
                and when do I rotate it</li
              >
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="problem-sidebar">
            <h4 class="sidebar-title">What I heard</h4>
            <p class="sidebar-story">
              Asked for the hardest part of rotating a key, one developer
              answered simply: "knowing where it's used." Another described
              being forced to write a lambda that renewed a Grafana key every 30
              days, because nothing else was tracking it. The incidents, when
              they happened, were the kind that "stop the world." The tooling
              existed; the honest, low-friction way to stay on top of it did
              not.
            </p>
            <p
              class="sidebar-story"
              style="margin-top: 24px; font-style: normal; color: #fff;"
            >
              This wasn't a tooling problem.<br />
              It was a design problem.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Research Section -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">03 — RESEARCH & DISCOVERY</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Understanding Developer Pain</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="research-visual">
          <img
            src="/images/keystrok/survey.png"
            alt="The API Key Rotation Practices survey I ran on Google Forms"
            class="survey-image"
            style="max-width: 460px; display: block; margin: 0 auto 16px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);"
          />
          <p class="image-caption"
            >A short survey on API key rotation practices, shared with dev
            friends and r/grafana</p
          >
        </div>

        <h3 style="font-size: 32px; margin-bottom: 32px;"
          >What the research surfaced</h3
        >
        <p class="section-description" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
          A small, honest study: a short survey plus conversations with a few
          developer friends and some folks on r/grafana. Tiny sample, but the
          answers pointed at the same thing again and again, and straight at the
          design problem.
        </p>

        <div class="stats-grid">
          <div class="stat-card">
            <div class="stat-value">No system</div>
            <div class="stat-label"
              >Nobody I asked had a systematic way to track where their keys
              were used</div
            >
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <div class="stat-value">"Where?"</div>
            <div class="stat-label"
              >The hardest part of rotation, in their words: knowing where a key
              is actually used</div
            >
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <div class="stat-value">Every one</div>
            <div class="stat-label"
              >Of the developers I heard from had hit an outage or incident tied
              to a rotation</div
            >
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <div class="stat-value">Months</div>
            <div class="stat-label"
              >How long keys routinely sit before anyone gets around to rotating
              them</div
            >
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 style="font-size: 32px; margin: 80px 0 32px;">Who I designed for</h3
        >
        <p class="section-description" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
          From those conversations, I built for three kinds of user:
        </p>

        <div class="segments-grid">
          <div class="segment-card">
            <h4 class="segment-title">Solo Developers</h4>
            <p class="segment-text"
              >Need simple, free solutions without complexity overhead</p
            >
          </div>
          <div class="segment-card">
            <h4 class="segment-title">Small Teams</h4>
            <p class="segment-text"
              >Want collaboration features without enterprise complexity</p
            >
          </div>
          <div class="segment-card">
            <h4 class="segment-title">Security-Conscious Startups</h4>
            <p class="segment-text"
              >Require audit trails but can't afford enterprise tools</p
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Design Strategy Section -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">04 — DESIGN STRATEGY</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Security Through Simplicity</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="principles-spread">
          <div class="principles-list">
            <div class="principle">
              <div class="principle-number">1</div>
              <div class="principle-content">
                <h4>10-Second Overview</h4>
                <p
                  >Critical information visible instantly. No drilling down
                  through menus to understand system health.</p
                >
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="principle">
              <div class="principle-number">2</div>
              <div class="principle-content">
                <h4>Progressive Disclosure</h4>
                <p
                  >Complexity available when needed, but never forced. Simple
                  tasks stay simple.</p
                >
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="principle">
              <div class="principle-number">3</div>
              <div class="principle-content">
                <h4>Proactive Security</h4>
                <p
                  >Guide users toward best practices through smart defaults and
                  gentle nudges.</p
                >
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="principle">
              <div class="principle-number">4</div>
              <div class="principle-content">
                <h4>Developer Aesthetics</h4>
                <p
                  >Dark mode first, information dense, terminal-inspired design
                  that feels familiar.</p
                >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="design-decisions">
            <h3>Key Design Decisions</h3>

            <div class="decision-item">
              <h4>Visual Hierarchy Through Color</h4>
              <p
                >Colour is reserved for security state, so the one thing that
                needs attention reads in a glance, before you parse a single
                word.</p
              >
              <div class="color-indicators">
                <span class="color-indicator"
                  ><span class="color-dot red"></span> Overdue (act now)</span
                >
                <span class="color-indicator"
                  ><span class="color-dot yellow"></span> Rotation due soon</span
                >
                <span class="color-indicator"
                  ><span class="color-dot green"></span> Healthy</span
                >
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="decision-item">
              <h4>Advisory, never automated</h4>
              <p
                >Keystrok ranks and walks you through each rotation, but it
                never rotates or revokes a key on its own. The irreversible
                actions stay in your hands, behind a confirm. No surprise
                revokes.</p
              >
            </div>

            <div class="decision-item">
              <h4>It never holds your secrets</h4>
              <p
                >Scans are read-only, findings are stored hashed and masked, and
                the optional AI assistant reasons over key metadata, never the
                values. A security tool earns trust by handling less, not more.</p
              >
            </div>

            <div class="decision-item">
              <h4>Honest about what it can't know</h4>
              <p
                >It never guesses a key's real age. Every rotation deadline is
                counted from when a key was discovered, not a made-up creation
                date. Better an honest "found 6 days ago" than a confident lie.</p
              >
            </div>

            <div class="decision-item">
              <h4>One clear next action per row</h4>
              <p
                >Every key surfaces its most likely next step instead of a menu,
                so the common path is one move, not a hunt.</p
              >
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Solution Design Section -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
